## Report
Validated the authorization-bypass finding. Static path review showed the mismatch: `parse_copy_source` strips an optional leading slash and percent-decodes bucket/key (`crates/server-http/src/http/request.rs:394-413`); CopyObject parses the source at `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:1449-1452` but passes the raw header into the policy context at `mod.rs:1533-1540`; UploadPartCopy does the same at `mod.rs:2763` and `mod.rs:2794`; the policy engine evaluates `s3:x-amz-copy-source` directly from `input.policy_context.copy_source` at `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz/policy.rs:122-123`. I added targeted PoC tests. The HTTP parser test proves `src/private/foo`, `/src/private/foo`, and `src/%70rivate/foo` all parse to bucket `src`, key `private/foo`. The core CopyObject PoC creates a destination policy with broad Allow plus explicit Deny on `StringLike s3:x-amz-copy-source = src/private/*`; canonical policy context `src/private/foo` is denied, while the same parsed source object succeeds when policy context contains `/src/private/foo` or `src/%70rivate/foo`, and the copied body equals the private source. The UploadPartCopy PoC shows the same canonical-denied/raw-alternate-allowed behavior for multipart copy. Command evidence: `cargo test -p server-http raw_copy_source_spellings_parse_to_same_source_object -- --nocapture` passed 1/1; `cargo test -p server-core autovalidator_ -- --nocapture` passed 2/2. Direct crash was not applicable for this auth-bypass class and the debug tests exited normally. Valgrind and gdb were unavailable; no nightly toolchain was installed for ASan. I used lldb non-interactively on the CopyObject PoC test and broke at the raw policy-context construction, observing `raw_header` values `/src/private/foo` and `src/%70rivate/foo`; the test then exited status 0.

